# XSH atexit()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Each test forks a child that registers handlers and ends in the way under
# test; the handlers report through a pipe, and the parent judges. A handler
# only writes bytes (write() is async-signal-safe and needs no stdio).

assertion  XSH.atexit.001
anchor     functions/atexit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/atexit.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       atexit() of a function returns 0, and when the process then
           calls exit() the function is called, once.
note       The process exit status the child gives to exit() is still
           what waitpid() reports (a handler does not change it).
test       t/001-exit-calls.c

assertion  XSH.atexit.002
anchor     functions/atexit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       A function registered with atexit() is called after the
           initial call to main() returns.
test       t/002-main-return.c

assertion  XSH.atexit.003
anchor     functions/atexit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A function registered with atexit() is called when the last
           thread of the process terminates, and not earlier: with a
           second thread still running, the main thread's pthread_exit()
           does not run it.
note       The "last thread termination" wording came with the ISO C
           alignment of Issue 8 (defect 1302).
test       t/003-last-thread.c

assertion  XSH.atexit.004
anchor     functions/atexit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/atexit.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       At least 32 different functions can be registered: 32
           registrations all return 0, and exit() calls all 32.
test       t/004-thirty-two.c

assertion  XSH.atexit.005
anchor     functions/atexit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/atexit.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       Every registration that returned 0 is honoured and none that
           returned a non-zero value is: after up to 4096 registrations
           of one function, exit() calls it exactly as many times as
           registrations succeeded.
note       Registering past the limit may succeed or fail; the test
           accepts both. It does not assume {ATEXIT_MAX} is exact (an
           implementation may have registered functions of its own).
test       t/005-count-matches.c
